            <ArticleTitle>Split right coronary artery: its definition and its territory.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>477-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report here, for perhaps the 1st time in the English-language literature, the extent of the territory fed by the anterior bifurcation of the (anomalous) split right coronary artery (RCA). A 64-year-old man presented with an occlusion of the anterior bifurcation of a split RCA--which resulted in an infarct that involved both the inferoseptal left ventricular wall and the anterior right ventricular free wall. Split RCA is the same anomaly as the improperly named &quot;double right coronary artery.&quot; In reality, there are not 2 RCAs, but only split portions of the posterior descending branch of the RCA, with 2 separate proximal courses.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
